      <h2>How a Declaration of Trust works in Rhode Island</h2>
      <p>Rhode Island has not adopted the Uniform Trust Code and relies on common law and scattered statutory provisions in Title 18 (Fiduciaries) of R.I. Gen. Laws. A declaration of trust in Rhode Island is the written instrument that creates a trust. The settlor states an intent to hold property for named beneficiaries and appoints a trustee to manage it. The trust must be in writing, signed by the settlor, and delivered to the trustee with intent to create a trust. Rhode Island does not have comprehensive statutory trust execution requirements, relying heavily on common law. Notarization is recommended though not required by the trust statute itself. The trust becomes active when it is funded: real property moves by a new deed to the trustee, and bank and brokerage accounts are retitled in the name of the trust or trustee. Rhode Island repealed the Rule Against Perpetuities in 1999, allowing perpetual trusts and dynasty trusts. When proof of the trust is needed for real property transactions, R.I. Gen. Laws Â§ 34-4-27</p>
    </div>
