      <p>Rhode Island operates under R.I. Gen. Laws Title 18 (Fiduciaries), relying on common law and scattered statutory provisions rather than a comprehensive trust code. Rhode Island has NOT adopted the Uniform Trust Code, making its trust law less codified and more dependent on judicial precedent than most other states. The closest analog to a certification of trust is R.I. Gen. Laws Â§ 34-4-27, which provides a real-property affidavit or memorandum of trust, not a general certification of trust. Rhode Island repealed the Rule Against Perpetuities in 1999, allowing perpetual trusts and dynasty trusts. The state repealed the centuries-old law prohibiting property owners from shielding assets in a trust from creditors in 1999. Rhode Island imposes state income tax on trust income for resident trusts at rates of 3.75% to 5.99%. The state has no state estate tax. The common law framework provides flexibility but less statutory certainty and guidance for trustees and beneficiaries.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

